00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
we're going to begin this hour with a  look at children and gun safety the  decision to charge the parents of the  alleged gunman in the oxford michigan  school shooting with involuntary  manslaughter has brought new attention  to the laws surrounding children's  access to firearms  gun safety advocates want gun owners to  be held criminally responsible in the  event that they fail to properly secure  their firearms and members of the  medical community are pushing for more  funding to treat gun injuries and deaths  as a public health emergency senior  medical correspondent that's dr tara  narula spoke with a trauma surgeon at  the center of that push along with three  parents who sadly lost children to  firearms tara this is such an important  conversation good morning to you good  morning gail those parents all say the  deaths of their children were  preventable gwen lacroix's son jonah  payne used an unlocked gun to take his  own life at age 17. kristen song's 15  year old son ethan was killed while  playing with a gun at a friend's house  mark barton's seven-year-old son daniel  died in the sandy hook school shooting  we were joined by dr chethan sathya  director of northwell health's center  for gun violence prevention  this isn't something that happened to us  this is our life now i often say that i  feel like my dna has been rewired since  i was shot to death waiting for the er  doctor to come in we had no idea ethan  had been shot i thought he had gotten  hit by a car i was afraid of it for a  long time because jonah struggled with  his mental health i didn't  fight hard enough i didn't take a stand  and say there are not going to be any  unlocked guns in my home the gun jonah  accessed was registered to gwen  lacroix's then husband who confirmed to  cbs news that it was stored unlocked and  unloaded in a separate part of the house  from the bullets and in compliance with  michigan law whether you're talking  about accidental injuries or firearm  related  suicide either one is an exploration for  a younger kid or another thing is an  impulsive behavior and why is it that we  have  you know mental health issues in other  countries like canada europe  are no different than here but the  lethality when you have access to a  firearm is just exponentially higher  firearms are the leading cause of death  ages one to 19 and two-thirds of those  deaths are suicides it's estimated 13  million kids in the u.s live in a home  with at least one firearm  my son and his best friend  were posting to social media on a fake  account and the detective said there was  probably a hundred kids who knew  your son and his best friend were  playing with guns and said nothing  and that is why it is our job  to protect our children from themselves  following ethan's death in 2018 the song  family successfully lobbied for a  connecticut state law strengthening  requirements around safe storage of  firearms and homes opponents in the  state legislature called ethan's law too  broad but it passed with bipartisan  support and a federal version has been  introduced i was just asking basically  to step up and and make sure that  people cannot get a hold of unsecured  guns eight children are they are dying  because they've gained access to a gun  30 states and the district of columbia  have passed child access prevention laws  like ethan's law a similar measure was  working its way through the michigan  legislature at the time of the oxford  school shooting do you think that this  is something that is not on most parents  radar  i think it depends on where you live  yeah there are families who live with  this every day as part of their reality  and  unfortunately they're not getting the  support and the attention that  those of us who have  suffered high profile  tragedies for more than 20 years  federally funded research into gun  injury prevention was prohibited in 2019  congress lifted that ban dr sathya is  among the first to receive nih funding  to study the root causes of gun injury  and death obviously this is an area that  was not funded for a very long time  what did that do to our understanding of  firearm safety if you look at the  leading causes of death whether there be  heart disease motor vehicle collisions  cancer i mean firearms have always been  at the top and they receive like less  than one percent of the funding of those  other diseases i feel  frustrated  that we continue to come forward with  our stories  and  the inaction  continues to make jonah's loss  even worse than it already is our  research right now that's funded by the  nih is focused on asking every single  patient who comes into our hospital  questions related to firearm injury risk  and so we're actually using a model that  we use for substance use where we  started asking everybody  that's what helped normalize those  conversations and then if you screen  positive for access  you're going to get gun locks firearm  safety counseling and if you screen  positive for violence risk you're going  to get violence interrupters community  resources to hopefully break that cycle  of violence how do you feel about  gun ownership i have no problem if  someone owns a gun i'm against  negligent reckless behavior i would like  to see  there be consequences for gun-owning  adults who allow children to  get hold of their guns and either  hurt themselves or someone else i mean  we work with a ton of gun owners  gun-owning parents are even more  appreciative of having that conversation  because they really understand the  importance of safety i've found that  most gun owners that i speak to  identify as responsible gun owners i  mean all three of us it's a safe storage  issue and all three of our our  situations the gun could have been  stored in the time it takes you to take  one breath what have you learned about  the incidents that you've prevented  through sandy hook promise school  shootings  mass casualty events that someone says  something over social media or in  passing to somebody in school  if that individual is trained in our  safe something program they know exactly  what to do and they can tell trusted  adults who can get that person uh help  how do we  humanize this  and keep the faces of your children  present and all the children that have  been lost so that  it's not just about statistics and  numbers i i always have pictures of my  little daniel to look at the face of my  sweet little seven-year-old boy who was  innocently in his first grade classroom  when he was shot to death  if that doesn't resonate with you  my youngest james was three when jonah  died  um and he doesn't remember jonah it's  very difficult to grow through that and  to understand  and to not have that big brother  to guide you i really walk people  through ethan's day i want them to this  really feel  what it's like to have your son tell you  about all his hopes and dreams and then  you know  an hour later he's dead i want people to  watch the er doctor literally slide down  the wall and put his head in his hands  and whisper your son is gone  as a pediatric trauma surgeon who's had  to walk into that waiting room and  deliver news  impossible news to families  how does hearing this make you feel i  mean this is you know a preventable  crisis that we are doing nothing about  makes you feel helpless what do you hope  the legacy of your  children will be take a few minutes  to learn about our children  get to know them a little bit and  hopefully folks will understand  what's at stake  and  what what can be lost i'm working with  my community and really building up our  health care system to face this public  health crisis in the same way that we've  met the pandemic you know i always tell  people we don't get vacation from this  we there's no hump day there's no tji  friday you all will go home to your  lives you'll kiss your children we're  going to go home to an empty bedroom  all four of our panelists describe the  tremendous ripple effects including the  significant disability and chronic  injuries that come from non-fatal  firearm injuries and the psychological  damage to entire communities and  families including thoughts of suicide  and despair first thank you to those  parents for talking so candidly about  something that is so painful i was very  surprised here in your piece the leading  cause of death for children between the  ages of 1 to 19. and i've talked to i've  seen parents who have been in situations  where they've lost someone so tragically  anybody they say it's not about moving  on it's about moving forward what do  these parents say about how they get  through their days they were all so  compelling so strong and you know  kristen said the trauma is truly intense  and i think for them the strength comes  from the idea that they might save  another child the strength comes from  honoring the children that they lost and  also being there for their surviving  children you know their children have  helped pull them out of the darkness in  fact kristen told me you know she was  suicidal at one point and her surviving  son evan looked at her and said mom you  know you're not going to hurt yourself  are you because if you check out i'm  going to be right after you and you know  she said in that moment i realized i had  to survive and fight for my children and  my family so the ripple affects the  concentric circles it just goes on  yeah and the point was made that there  is not a tension between storing it  safely and getting access for the  reasons you have a gun in the first  place exactly important exactly doctor  thank you very much

